WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
Where do the enzymes go?

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.760
This yeast mutant keeps making them at 37 °C.

3
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.220
But it releases little.

4
00:00:11.383 --> 00:00:17.263
Yeast secrete enzymes along a route: ER, then Golgi, then vesicles, then the cell

5
00:00:17.263 --> 00:00:22.223
surface. Small membrane spheres called vesicles carry the cargo.

6
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:25.357
This mutant is called sec1.

7
00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:29.137
At 25 °C it grows and secretes.

8
00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:34.597
At 37 °C, its enzyme pool inside rises up to eightfold.

9
00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:40.737
The enzymes sit in membrane-bound vesicles that fail to fuse with the surface.

10
00:00:42.277 --> 00:00:46.797
Back at 25 °C, with protein synthesis blocked, the cells secrete them.

11
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:50.567
Every vesicle on that route has three jobs.

12
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:53.747
Budding: pick up cargo and pinch off.

13
00:00:55.147 --> 00:00:56.947
Targeting: be caught by the right membrane.

14
00:00:58.247 --> 00:00:59.667
Fusion: merge with it.

15
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.600
Back to sec1. For the last vesicle on the route, which job worked?

16
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.880
And which could have failed?

17
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:16.543
Budding worked, because the vesicles exist.

18
00:01:18.003 --> 00:01:19.243
Targeting or fusion failed.

19
00:01:20.563 --> 00:01:23.223
A pile of vesicles alone cannot tell you which.

20
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:29.707
Budding first. A coat of proteins assembles on a patch of membrane.

21
00:01:31.107 --> 00:01:34.947
Think of a cookie cutter with hands: it bends a bud and grabs the cargo.

22
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.100
At the ER this coat is COPII.

23
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.820
A switch called Sar1, holding GTP, pulls it on.

24
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.500
When Sar1 hydrolyzes that GTP, the coat begins to let go.

25
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:55.330
In a test tube, vesicles made with a GTP analogue that cannot be hydrolyzed still

26
00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.730
budded. But they kept their coat, and failed to target and fuse.

27
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.070
Making a vesicle and delivering it are separate steps.

28
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.650
Here is the one idea.

29
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.710
A vesicle's trip is three handoffs, and each can fail on its own.

30
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.910
A coat buds it. A Rab and a tether catch it.

31
00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.790
SNAREs, guided by an SM protein, fuse it.

32
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:26.590
Targeting next. A Rab is a small switch with a lipid tail.

33
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.810
Holding GDP, it can be carried in the cytosol by GDI.

34
00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:38.150
At the right membrane, an exchange factor loads GTP, and the Rab holds on.

35
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.100
Rab with GTP is one lit sign over a loading bay.

36
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.640
It recruits tethers, which catch the vesicle.

37
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.560
It is one sign among several.

38
00:02:50.633 --> 00:02:57.073
Fusion last. Two membranes resist merging, so SNARE proteins supply the pull.

39
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:03.013
Four strands, called Qa, Qb, Qc and R, come from the two membranes.

40
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:07.227
They zip into one rope, toward the membrane anchors.

41
00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:11.707
The zipping pulls the two bilayers together until they fuse.

42
00:03:13.087 --> 00:03:14.847
The zip itself uses no ATP.

43
00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:20.567
In the main fusion steps studied, SNAREs also need an SM protein.

44
00:03:21.907 --> 00:03:24.647
It binds them and helps the right four zip together.

45
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.630
Sec1 is the SM protein for the last step of secretion.

46
00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.090
That step is fusion of secretory vesicles with the plasma membrane.

47
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:40.213
So at 37 °C, vesicles still bud and pile up.

48
00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:45.193
Back at 25 °C, Sec1 works again, and the stored vesicles fuse.

49
00:03:46.383 --> 00:03:48.723
But the pile alone never named fusion.

50
00:03:50.023 --> 00:03:53.783
A failed Rab, tether or SNARE for that step gives the same pile.

51
00:03:55.263 --> 00:03:56.703
Sec1's known job names it.

52
00:03:57.633 --> 00:04:01.293
On a linear route, cargo piles up in the last place it reached.

53
00:04:02.653 --> 00:04:05.733
Double mutants used that rule to put the steps in order.

54
00:04:06.817 --> 00:04:08.397
Test tubes add timing.

55
00:04:09.837 --> 00:04:15.037
GDI, which pulls GDP-bound Rabs off membranes, blocks fusion when added at the

56
00:04:15.037 --> 00:04:18.617
start. What if it is added after vesicles dock?

57
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:31.540
Its effect fades. Once vesicles had docked, fusion was no longer sensitive to GDI.

58
00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:37.240
A fast, specific inhibitor stops working once the state it targets is past.

59
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:43.753
So each vesicle on this route relies on three separate handoffs: bud, catch, fuse.

60
00:04:45.173 --> 00:04:46.593
Any one can fail on its own.

61
00:04:47.793 --> 00:04:52.093
Where cargo stalls, and when an inhibitor stops mattering, help place the break.
